WB.L("ibn-battuta", "explore", "intermediate", "Ibn Battuta: the greatest traveller of the Middle Ages",
  "In 1325 a young Moroccan set off on a pilgrimage to Mecca. He came home almost a quarter of a century later, having seen more of the world than anyone of his age.",
  [
    ["Leaving Tangier", "Ibn Battuta left Morocco at 21 on the pilgrimage to Mecca and kept travelling for decades.",
      "In June 1325, a 21-year-old named Ibn Battuta left his home in Tangier, in what is now Morocco, to make the hajj, the pilgrimage to Mecca that Muslims aim to complete at least once in their lives. He came from a family of legal scholars and was trained in Islamic law. He set out alone, and later described feeling such grief at leaving his parents that he wept. He expected the journey to take perhaps a year or two. Instead, he would not return home for around 24 years. His travels took him across North Africa, the Middle East, East Africa, Central Asia, India, South-East Asia and, by his account, China, a distance often estimated at well over 100,000 kilometres."],
    ["A world connected by faith", "A shared religion, language and legal system let Ibn Battuta travel and find work far from home.",
      "How could a young man travel so far in the 1300s? Much of the answer lies in the world he lived in. Islam had spread across a vast region, from Spain and West Africa to India and parts of South-East Asia. Across this world, educated Muslims shared Arabic as a language of learning, followed the same broad body of Islamic law and recognised each other's scholarship. A trained legal scholar like Ibn Battuta could arrive in a distant city and be welcomed by rulers and religious leaders, who often gave travellers gifts, lodging and sometimes jobs. Pilgrim caravans, trade routes and religious lodges provided support along the way. It was a network as much as a map."],
    ["From Mecca to the coast of Africa", "After his first pilgrimage he explored the Middle East and sailed down the East African coast.",
      "Ibn Battuta reached Mecca in 1326, but rather than heading home he kept exploring. He travelled through Iraq and Persia, returned to Mecca, and spent time studying there. Around 1330, he sailed down the Red Sea and along the coast of East Africa, visiting trading cities such as Mogadishu, Mombasa and Kilwa. He described the wealth of these ports, which traded gold, ivory and other goods across the Indian Ocean. He later journeyed through Anatolia, in modern Turkey, and onto the steppes of Central Asia, where he joined the court of a Mongol khan. He says he travelled with one of the khan's wives, a Byzantine princess, to visit Constantinople, the great Christian capital."],
    ["Judge in Delhi", "He spent years serving the unpredictable Sultan of Delhi as a judge.",
      "Ibn Battuta's next goal was India, where the Sultan of Delhi, Muhammad bin Tughluq, was famous for his generosity to foreign scholars. After a hard journey through Afghanistan, he arrived in the 1330s and was appointed a qadi, or judge, with a large salary. He stayed for about eight years. But the Sultan was also notorious for sudden cruelty, and courtiers could fall from favour without warning. Ibn Battuta himself fell under suspicion at one point and feared for his life. So when the Sultan chose him to lead an embassy to the Mongol emperor of China, he must have been relieved. The journey began badly: his party was attacked, and the ships meant to carry the gifts were wrecked."],
    ["Islands and the road to China", "He worked in the Maldives, visited Sri Lanka and says he reached China.",
      "Having lost the embassy, and afraid to return to Delhi, Ibn Battuta made his way to the Maldives. There he served as a judge for around a year, married several times and tried, with mixed success, to impose stricter religious law on the islanders. He then visited Sri Lanka, where he climbed the sacred mountain known as Adam's Peak, before continuing to Bengal and South-East Asia. By his account he finally reached China in the 1340s, describing its ports, paper money and porcelain. Some historians doubt how far into China he really went, because parts of this section are vague or seem borrowed from other sources. Even so, his descriptions show a world joined by busy sea trade."],
    ["Home, then across the Sahara", "Back in Morocco, he set out again, crossing the Sahara to the Mali Empire.",
      "Ibn Battuta returned to Morocco in 1349, only to learn that his mother had died of the plague that was sweeping the region. Remarkably, he was soon travelling again. He visited Granada in Muslim Spain, then in the early 1350s joined a camel caravan across the Sahara to the Mali Empire, one of the richest states in Africa, famous for its gold. He visited the court of the ruler, Mansa Sulayman, and travelled to Timbuktu. He admired the safety of travel in Mali and the devotion of its people to prayer, but was disappointed by the gifts he received and disapproved of some local customs, revealing his own biases as clearly as anything about the places he visited."],
    ["The book he dictated", "His travels survive in the Rihla, a book dictated to a court scholar.",
      "When Ibn Battuta finally settled in Morocco, the Sultan of Fez asked him to record his journeys. He dictated his memories to a young scholar named Ibn Juzayy, who shaped them into a book completed around 1355. Its title is long, but it is usually just called the Rihla, meaning journey. Historians treat it with care. It was written from memory years later, some passages appear to be copied from earlier writers, and a few journeys may be exaggerated. Yet it remains one of the richest records of the fourteenth century world, describing food, customs, rulers and trade from Spain to Asia. Ibn Battuta is still celebrated in Morocco, and even a crater on the Moon bears his name."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did Ibn Battuta first leave Tangier in 1325?", ["To trade spices", "To make the pilgrimage to Mecca", "To escape a war", "To study astronomy in China"], 1, "He set out on the hajj, then kept travelling for decades."],
    [1, "What helped Ibn Battuta find work and welcome so far from home?", ["Royal letters from the King of France", "His fluency in Chinese", "A shared religion, legal tradition and language of learning", "A guild of professional explorers"], 2, "As a trained legal scholar, he was valued across the Muslim world."],
    [3, "What role did Ibn Battuta hold in Delhi?", ["Army general", "Court poet", "Treasurer", "Judge"], 3, "He served the Sultan Muhammad bin Tughluq as a qadi for about eight years."],
    [5, "Which wealthy African empire did he visit after crossing the Sahara?", ["The Mali Empire", "The Kingdom of Aksum", "The Zulu Kingdom", "Great Zimbabwe"], 0, "He visited the court of Mansa Sulayman and travelled to Timbuktu."],
    [6, "Why do historians read the Rihla with care?", ["It was written in code", "It was dictated from memory years later and borrows some passages", "Most copies were destroyed", "It was written by his enemies"], 1, "Despite its flaws, it remains a rich record of the fourteenth century world."]
  ]);

WB.L("photography-invention", "explore", "beginner", "How photography was invented",
  "For thousands of years, people could watch an image of the world appear in a darkened room but had no way to keep it. Then chemistry caught up.",
  [
    ["A picture in a dark room", "The camera obscura projected images long before anyone could capture them.",
      "If you darken a room completely and make a small hole in one wall, an upside-down image of the outside world appears on the opposite wall. This effect, called the camera obscura, Latin for dark room, was described by thinkers in ancient China and Greece and studied carefully by the Arab scholar Ibn al-Haytham around 1,000 years ago. By the Renaissance, portable versions with lenses and mirrors helped artists trace scenes accurately. But the image vanished the moment the light changed. Capturing it permanently needed a material that changed when light hit it, and a way to stop that change once the picture was made. Solving those two problems took until the nineteenth century."],
    ["The first photograph", "Nicéphore Niépce made the oldest surviving camera photograph in the 1820s, with an exposure of many hours.",
      "In about 1826 or 1827, the French inventor Nicéphore Niépce placed a pewter plate coated with a kind of bitumen in a camera at a window of his country house. Bitumen hardens when exposed to light. After a very long exposure, probably many hours and possibly days, he washed away the unhardened parts, leaving a faint image of buildings and rooftops. Called View from the Window at Le Gras, it is the oldest surviving photograph taken with a camera. It is now held at the University of Texas in Austin. Because the sun moved across the sky during the exposure, buildings on both sides of the scene appear lit, an eerie clue to how slow the process was."],
    ["The daguerreotype", "Louis Daguerre's 1839 process made sharp, unique images on silvered copper plates.",
      "Niépce teamed up with Louis Daguerre, a Parisian showman famous for theatrical scenery. After Niépce died in 1833, Daguerre kept experimenting and developed a much faster process. A silver-coated copper plate was made light-sensitive with iodine fumes, exposed in a camera, then developed with mercury vapour, which was later recognised as highly toxic. The result was a stunningly detailed image, but each one was unique and could not be copied. In 1839 the French government acquired the rights and announced the process to the world, granting Daguerre and Niépce's son pensions. Daguerreotype studios soon opened across Europe and America. One of Daguerre's street scenes captured what are often called the first people ever photographed: a man having his boots polished."],
    ["Negatives and copies", "William Henry Fox Talbot's paper negatives allowed many prints from one image.",
      "In England, William Henry Fox Talbot was working on his own method at the same time. He used paper soaked in silver salts, which darkened in light, to make negatives, images where light and dark were reversed. By shining light through a negative onto another sheet of sensitive paper, he could make a positive print, and he could do it again and again. His improved process, the calotype, was patented in 1841. Calotypes were softer and less detailed than daguerreotypes, but the negative and positive principle was the future. Almost all film photography for the next 150 years followed Talbot's idea. One of his earliest surviving negatives, from 1835, shows a window at his home, Lacock Abbey."],
    ["Photography spreads", "Faster processes brought photography to wars, families and colonial Australia.",
      "In 1851 Frederick Scott Archer introduced the wet collodion process, using glass plates that combined fine detail with the ability to make copies. Exposures dropped to seconds. Photographers had to coat, expose and develop each plate while it was still wet, so they hauled portable darkrooms with them. Photography soon recorded war, from the Crimean War to the American Civil War, bringing distant conflict home to the public in a new way. Portrait studios spread rapidly, and many families could afford a likeness for the first time. In Australia, a daguerreotype of the Sydney politician and doctor William Bland, taken in 1845, is thought to be the oldest surviving photograph made in the country."],
    ["You press the button", "George Eastman's Kodak camera turned photography into a hobby for everyone.",
      "Photography was still fiddly and expensive until an American businessman, George Eastman, made it simple. In 1888 his company launched the Kodak camera, sold already loaded with a roll of film for about 100 pictures. When it was full, customers posted the whole camera back to the company, which developed the prints, reloaded the camera and returned it. The famous slogan was 'You press the button, we do the rest.' In 1900 Kodak released the Brownie, a simple box camera priced at one dollar in the United States, putting photography within reach of children and ordinary families. Holiday snaps, birthday photos and the family album were born, and taking pictures became part of everyday life."],
    ["Colour, then digital", "Colour film arrived in the twentieth century, then digital sensors changed everything.",
      "Early colour photography was difficult. The French Lumière brothers released the Autochrome process in 1907, which used dyed grains of potato starch to produce dreamy colour images on glass. Easier colour film, such as Kodachrome, launched in 1935. Then came a bigger shift. In 1975 Kodak engineer Steven Sasson built an experimental digital camera. It was about the size of a toaster, captured black and white images of just 0.01 megapixels and recorded them onto cassette tape. Kodak did not push the technology hard, partly because film was so profitable, and the company filed for bankruptcy protection in 2012. Today billions of people carry a camera in their phone, and more photos are taken every year than in all earlier history."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does camera obscura mean?", ["Hidden lens", "Dark room", "Painted light", "Small window"], 1, "The Latin phrase describes the darkened room in which an image is projected."],
    [1, "Why do buildings on both sides of Niépce's first photograph look sunlit?", ["He used two cameras", "He painted the shadows out", "The exposure lasted so long that the sun moved across the sky", "It was taken at noon in summer"], 2, "The exposure took many hours, possibly days."],
    [2, "What was a major limitation of the daguerreotype?", ["Each image was unique and could not be copied", "It only worked at night", "It needed a huge glass lens", "It could only be used outdoors"], 0, "The detail was stunning, but there was no negative to make prints from."],
    [3, "What was Fox Talbot's key contribution?", ["The first colour photograph", "The roll film camera", "The digital sensor", "A negative that could make many positive prints"], 3, "His negative and positive principle guided film photography for 150 years."],
    [5, "What did Kodak's slogan promise customers in 1888?", ["Photos in one minute", "You press the button, we do the rest", "A camera in every pocket", "Colour for everyone"], 1, "Customers sent the camera back and Kodak developed the film for them."]
  ]);

WB.L("bicycle", "explore", "beginner", "The bicycle: a simple machine that changed society",
  "Two wheels, a frame and a chain. The bicycle looks so obvious it is hard to believe it took the best part of a century to get right, or that it helped change the lives of women.",
  [
    ["A running machine", "The first two-wheeler, built by Karl Drais in 1817, had no pedals at all.",
      "In 1817 a German forestry official named Karl Drais rode a strange new machine near the city of Mannheim. It had two wheels in a line, a wooden frame and a seat, and he pushed it along by striding with his feet on the ground, much like a modern children's balance bike. He called it a Laufmaschine, or running machine, and it became known as the draisine or dandy horse. Some historians link the invention to 1816, the so-called Year Without a Summer, when a huge volcanic eruption in Indonesia had disrupted the climate and crop failures made feeding horses expensive. The craze for dandy horses faded quickly, but the idea of balancing on two wheels had arrived."],
    ["Pedals and boneshakers", "In the 1860s, French makers added pedals to the front wheel.",
      "Nearly half a century passed before the next big step. In the 1860s in France, makers attached pedals and cranks directly to the front wheel, so riders could keep their feet off the ground. Who did it first is disputed, with credit usually given to the Michaux family business in Paris or to a mechanic named Pierre Lallement. These machines, called velocipedes, had iron frames and wooden wheels with iron rims. On the rough roads of the day they rattled so badly that English speakers nicknamed them boneshakers. Even so, they were wildly popular for a time. Riding schools opened, races were held and people began to imagine bicycles as practical transport rather than a toy."],
    ["The penny-farthing", "Giant front wheels made bikes faster but dangerously high.",
      "With pedals fixed to the front wheel, each turn of the pedals moved the bike only as far as that wheel's circumference. The way to go faster was to make the front wheel bigger. By the 1870s, high wheelers had front wheels well over a metre across, with a tiny wheel behind. Later they were nicknamed penny-farthings, after the large penny and small farthing coins of Britain. Solid rubber tyres and wire spokes made them lighter and smoother than boneshakers, and they were fast. But the rider sat high above the front wheel, and hitting a rock or pothole could pitch them head first over the handlebars. Riding one was an athletic, risky hobby mostly for young men."],
    ["The safety bicycle", "Chain drive and equal wheels in the 1880s created the bicycle we know today.",
      "The breakthrough came in 1885, when the English inventor John Kemp Starley launched the Rover. It had two wheels of similar size and a chain driving the back wheel. Gears in the chain drive meant a small wheel could still travel fast, and the rider sat low enough to put a foot down. It was called a safety bicycle, and its basic layout is still used today. A few years later came comfort. In 1888 John Boyd Dunlop, a Scottish vet living in Belfast, developed an air-filled tyre for his young son's tricycle. Pneumatic tyres made riding far smoother and faster, and Dunlop's name became a famous brand. By the 1890s, the modern bicycle was essentially complete."],
    ["Freedom on two wheels", "The 1890s bicycle boom gave many women new independence.",
      "The 1890s saw a worldwide bicycle craze, and its effects went far beyond transport. For many women, the bicycle offered a new kind of freedom: the ability to travel independently, without a chaperone or a horse and carriage. Cycling also encouraged dress reform. Heavy, long skirts and tight corsets were impractical on a bike, and some women adopted divided skirts or loose trousers called bloomers, to the outrage of critics. The American campaigner for women's voting rights Susan B. Anthony said in an 1896 interview that the bicycle had done more to emancipate women than anything else in the world. The bicycle became a symbol of the 'New Woman', confident, active and moving about on her own terms."],
    ["Bikes in the Australian bush", "In the 1890s, Australians cycled across the outback and around the continent.",
      "Australia caught the bicycle craze in the 1890s, and bikes were put to surprisingly tough work. During the gold rushes in Western Australia, cyclists carried messages and mail across long distances to and from the goldfields. Shearers in the outback rode between sheep stations, and some cyclists made epic overland journeys, including rides around the whole continent, on dirt tracks with no support. Bikes were cheaper than horses and did not need feed or water, a big advantage in dry country. Later, cycling became a popular sport, and Hubert Opperman became a national hero for his endurance riding in the 1920s and 1930s. In 1990 Victoria became the first place in the world to make bicycle helmets compulsory."],
    ["Small machine, big legacy", "Bicycle technology helped give rise to cars and aeroplanes, and bikes are still among the most efficient ways to travel.",
      "The bicycle's influence spread into other machines. Bicycle makers developed and refined ball bearings, chain drives, pneumatic tyres and lightweight steel tubing, all of which fed into early cars and motorcycles. The campaign for better roads in some countries began with cyclists, before motorists took it over. Most famously, Wilbur and Orville Wright ran a bicycle shop in Dayton, Ohio, and used its skills, tools and profits to build the aeroplane they flew in 1903. Today the bicycle remains one of the most energy-efficient ways for a person to travel. In cities from Amsterdam to Copenhagen, bikes are used for a large share of daily trips, and cities around the world are building bike lanes to follow their lead."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How did riders move Karl Drais's 1817 running machine?", ["With hand cranks", "By pushing along the ground with their feet", "With pedals on the back wheel", "With a small steam engine"], 1, "The draisine had no pedals, like a modern balance bike."],
    [2, "Why did penny-farthings have such large front wheels?", ["To make them easier to climb on", "To fit a bigger seat", "So each pedal turn covered more distance", "To carry luggage"], 2, "With pedals fixed to the front wheel, a bigger wheel meant more speed."],
    [3, "What did John Boyd Dunlop develop in 1888?", ["The air-filled tyre", "The chain drive", "The bicycle bell", "Hand brakes"], 0, "He made a pneumatic tyre for his son's tricycle, making rides far smoother."],
    [4, "How did the bicycle change life for many women in the 1890s?", ["It was banned for women everywhere", "It replaced sewing as a hobby", "It was mainly used for deliveries", "It offered independent travel and encouraged dress reform"], 3, "Bikes gave women new freedom and helped loosen restrictive clothing."],
    [6, "Which famous inventors ran a bicycle shop?", ["The Lumière brothers", "Wilbur and Orville Wright", "Karl Benz and Gottlieb Daimler", "Thomas Edison and Nikola Tesla"], 1, "The Wright brothers used their bicycle shop's skills to build their aeroplane."]
  ]);
